From this session's dedicated Reputation Manager audit (all 7 workers) and
Whop Agent audit part 1 (8 of 15 workers) — both came back clean at the
execution-safety level; fixes here are all display/documentation accuracy,
no run-blocking or live-action-risk bugs found in either product.

Reputation Manager (worker-registry.ts):
- activeEngines: tier corrected deferrable -> visible-default. null is
  engine-panel-service.ts's permanently-valid "check every configured
  engine" default (the majority real-world state, since the intake form's
  own default saves back as null), not a state that becomes blocking —
  "deferrable" mischaracterized it.
- operatorDomains: tier corrected hidden-default -> visible-default
  (cosmetic only, no functional consequence) — it's rendered as a plain,
  always-visible main-flow field, not gated behind an Advanced toggle the
  way "hidden-default" implies.
- "AI Engine Watch" and "Crisis Response" capabilities: dropped
  activeEngines / operatorPagePhone / crisisThresholdOverride from their
  requiredFieldKeys — all three have real, always-applied fallbacks, so
  requiring them made the Live Capability Matrix show both capabilities as
  permanently "not fully active" for the large majority of real clients
  despite both workers being 100% functional on their documented defaults.

Whop Agent (worker-config-completeness.ts header):
- Corrected a real documentation-accuracy gap: the module header claimed
  whop-connect and whop-cancellation-save-offer were "covered" by this
  file's CHECKERS map. This session's audit traced every dispatch call
  site in the repo and confirmed neither worker ever runs through
  executeSkillRun (src/inngest/skill.ts) at all — both dispatch entirely
  off their own route/webhook path, making their CHECKERS entries dead
  code at runtime. Both are still genuinely protected (whop-connect's own
  route validates before writing; whop-cancellation-save-offer's real
  dispatch site has an inline guard byte-for-byte equivalent to the
  checker, plus every actual Whop write is human-approval-queued
  regardless) — documented explicitly instead of leaving the header
  overstating what's actually gating these two workers.

tsc clean. Whop Agent audit part 2 (remaining 7 workers) still in flight —
follow-up commit once it lands.

