From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 20:49:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Whop Agent audit part 2: fix disabled-skill bypass on 3
 write-capable workers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The most severe finding across all 4 worker audits this session: this
session's dedicated Whop Agent audit (final 7 workers) found that
whop-ads-draft-approve, whop-bulk-promo-codes, and whop-dispute-response's
manual on-demand path could all take a real, live action against a
client's connected account with the skill explicitly TOGGLED OFF in the
dashboard — only the webhook-auto-trigger paths in src/inngest/whop-agent.ts
checked isSkillEnabledForEngagement; the direct dashboard API routes and
the Teammates-chat wrappers never did. Worst case: whop-ads-draft-approve
generates real, billable Meta ad media (media generation happens before
any human approval — only the flip-to-active spend step is gated) through
either path with zero enablement check anywhere along it.

Fixed by adding the same isSkillEnabledForEngagement check every webhook
handler already uses, everywhere it was missing:
- src/lib/chat-whop-agent.ts: new requireSkillEnabled helper, wired into
  draftWhopAdForEngagement, flipWhopAdActiveForEngagement,
  runBulkPromoCodesForEngagement, assembleDisputeResponseForEngagement,
  submitDisputeEvidenceForEngagement.
- The 3 direct dashboard routes (whop-agent/ads-draft, bulk-promo-codes,
  dispute-response) — both POST and PUT/queue-time entry points.
- 3 approval-gate.ts executors (whop_bulk_promo_codes_confirm,
  whop_dispute_evidence_submit, whop_ads_flip_to_active) now re-check at
  approval time too, not just at the original request — same pattern
  webhook_enrollment's executor already established, since the skill can
  be turned off in the window between an action being queued and a human
  approving it.

Also documented (worker-config-completeness.ts header) 2 further real
findings from this audit and part 1: whop-bridge-manager's CHECKERS entry
is dead code at runtime (dispatches only through its own webhook handler,
never executeSkillRun) — verified correct logic, no regression, just
unreachable; and whop-connect/whop-cancellation-save-offer's entries are
the same. All 3 are still genuinely protected by other mechanisms, traced
and confirmed per-worker.

No worker in either Whop Agent audit batch can take a live action with
missing/garbage PERSISTENT CONFIG — every write path is human-approval-
gated, dry-run-by-default, or throws cleanly on a missing connection. The
bug fixed here was specifically the disabled-skill bypass, not a
config-completeness gap.

tsc clean, vitest matches established baseline exactly (59 failed/210
passed/18 skipped, all pre-existing test-harness noise unrelated to this
work).

