From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 20:59:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Close the Whop Agent UI treatment gap: whop-connect,
 whop-cancellation-save-offer, whop-bridge-manager
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

These 3 were the worst miss flagged in review: real configured workers
(whop-connect is Whop Agent's own onboarding worker — the first screen a
client ever sees) that had received none of the Phase 3/5 treatment every
other configured worker on the platform already had.

- whop-connect-config-form.tsx: wrapped the API key input in the same
  vault-slot card styling used elsewhere, added a real "Armed & Watching"
  state-shift banner on connect (previously just plain text), and
  reframed the existing scope-probe results as a capability matrix
  (X/Y active count, matching WorkerCapabilityMatrix's own visual
  language) instead of a bare list.
- WHOP_AGENT_CONFIG_FIELDS had zero tier data anywhere (confirmed by this
  session's own Whop Agent audit) despite whop-cancellation-save-offer and
  whop-bridge-manager both having real blocking fields collected by a real
  form. Added tiers matching each field's actual description ("Blocks" /
  "does not block"), a WORKER_CAPABILITIES entry for each, and a
  worker-capability-status.ts reader for each — same pattern every other
  configured worker already has.
- Both forms now use ProgressiveFlow (offer terms vs. guardrails;
  destination vs. field mapping) instead of a flat field stack, with
  WorkerCapabilityMatrix wired in. All state, load effects, and save()
  payloads are unchanged — pure layout/treatment retrofit, same approach
  Phase 3's icp-lock/pre-call-read/pin-down retrofits used.

tsc clean after each file.

